Phase 2: rotation, decided by measurement

The riskiest part of the track. Research said arbitrary rotation of a sprite
this small may simply not be possible, and the plan said the decision would be
measured rather than argued.

Four routes built, rendered on a five-by-twelve limb and a sixteen-by-
twenty-four figure at six angles, and scored against a thirty-twofold detour
standing in for the true turn.

  route                       worst agreement         cost per figure turn
  native grid                 94 percent              0.2 ms
  eightfold detour            98-99 percent           37 ms
  sixteenfold detour          99-100 percent          149 ms
  snap to quarter turns       20-36 percent           0.1 ms

Chosen: the eightfold detour, with quarter turns taking the exact path since
they are free. Sixteenfold buys under a point for four times the cost.
Snapping does not rotate at all below forty-five degrees, which is what its
score says.

Budget: a ten-frame animation with six limb-sized parts is about sixty turns
at roughly six milliseconds, so under half a second behind a menu command.

Two measurements failed before one worked, and both are kept in the tool.
Counting holes surrounded on four sides found none anywhere. Counting how many
separate pieces the drawing broke into found none either. I had read the
comparison sheet as showing a bad turn snapping the legs off; that was wrong,
and the metrics said so before it went into a document as a fact. The damage
is edge quality, not topology.

Guarantees held throughout: no route blends, so no colour is invented - the
enlargement copies from neighbours, the turn takes one source pixel per
destination pixel, the reduction votes. Ties resolve the same way every run,
so the same input always gives the same picture.

Gates: 164 unit tests pass, 29 runtime checks pass, linter and formatter clean
across 42 files, core layer clean, core coverage 100 percent.
